    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_perfect_graph - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_perfect_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_perfect_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if an undirected graph is perfect.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_perfect_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if an undirected graph is perfect.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_perfect_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Check if an undirected graph is perfect.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if an undirected graph is perfect.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L33725-L33830){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_perfect_graph()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is perfect graph](../assets/graphs/is-perfect-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: the house, a cycle of five nodes A, B, C, D, E with the chord B to E, which splits it into a triangle and a square; it has no odd hole and no odd antihole, so is_perfect_graph() returns True. Right: the five-cycle without the chord (the dashed edge) is itself an odd hole, a chordless cycle of length 5 (in the accent color), so is_perfect_graph() returns False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA graph is *perfect* when, in it and in every induced subgraph, the chromatic number\nequals the size of the largest clique: the only thing forcing extra colors is a clique\nthat needs them. Bipartite graphs, chordal graphs, comparability graphs, interval\ngraphs and the complements of all of these are perfect. On perfect graphs maximum\ncliques, maximum independent sets and optimal colorings can be found in polynomial\ntime, problems that are NP-hard in general.\n\nBy the Strong Perfect Graph Theorem (Chudnovsky, Robertson, Seymour and Thomas, 2006),\na graph is perfect exactly when it contains no *odd hole* (an induced cycle of odd\nlength 5 or more) and no *odd antihole* (the complement of one). This is what is\nchecked. The five-cycle is the smallest imperfect graph: its largest clique is an edge,\nyet it needs three colors.\n\nParallel edges count once and self-loops are ignored.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if the graph is perfect. Graphs with at most four nodes always are.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is directed.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nBipartite and chordal graphs are accepted immediately, since neither can contain an\nodd hole or antihole. Otherwise a depth-first search grows induced paths from each\nnode, extending only by nodes adjacent to the path's last node and to nothing else on\nit, and stops at the first odd induced cycle of length 5 or more; the same search then\nruns on the complement. Each cycle is searched for only from its smallest node, so the\nsearch is fast on the graphs met in practice, but it is exponential in the worst case\n(the polynomial recognition algorithm of Chudnovsky et al., 2005, runs in O(n⁹) and is\nnot practical). Results match NetworkX's `is_perfect_graph()`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_chordal`) \n\n:   Check for chordless cycles of four or more nodes.\n\n[](`~clique_number`) \n\n:   The size of a largest clique.\n\n[](`~greedy_color`) \n\n:   Color the nodes so that no edge joins two of a color.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nEven cycles are bipartite and so perfect, and odd cycles of five or more nodes are odd\nholes:\n\n::: {#c3ebe389 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\n{n: ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(n).is_perfect_graph() for n in range(3, 9)}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{3: True, 4: True, 5: False, 6: True, 7: False, 8: True}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA seven-cycle's complement is an odd antihole, so it isn't perfect either, though it has\nno odd hole:\n\n::: {#946e3561 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(7).complement().is_perfect_graph()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\nFalse\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### More slots than the largest clique\n\n`exams()` joins two of a made-up college's courses when some student takes both, so\ntheir exams can't share a time slot. Four slots are enough, and four courses all clash,\nso the timetable needs exactly as many slots as its largest clique. But the graph isn't\nperfect:\n\n::: {#f2f7bf3d .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\nexams.is_perfect_graph()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\nFalse\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nPhilosophy, Literature, Economics, History and Biology form a cycle of five with no\nchords, an odd hole. No three of them all clash, yet they need three slots:\n\n::: {#ee1eaebf .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nhole = [\"Philosophy\", \"Literature\", \"Economics\", \"History\", \"Biology\"]\npart = exams.subgraph([exams.get_id_from_label(c) for c in hole])\n\nslots = part.greedy_color(strategy=\"dsatur\")\npart.clique_number(), len(set(slots.values()))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(2, 3)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#538f2d9b .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams.plot(\n    highlight=[*hole, hole[0]], size_by=\"students\", label_by=\"label\",\n    seed=3, height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], b